 "interpretationRules": {
  "encodedRules": [],
  "sourceGuidance": [
   {
    "title": "PARDI-AR-Q Scoring & Interpretation",
    "text": "The PARDI-AR-Q is originally described as a 32-item scale, however items 1, 2 and 3 are date, date of birth and sex, which are recorded automatically in the NovoPsych platform, therefore, NovoPsych refers to the PARDI-AR-Q as being a 29-item scale, numbering these items 1 to 29 in the order they are answered, beginning with height and weight. Question numbers on this page match the 29-item formatting.\nThe 29 items fall into three blocks:\n1. Anthropometric items (items 1-2): the child’s height and weight (2 items). Together with age and sex, these produce BMI and growth status.\n2. Diagnostic items (items 3 to 18): twelve are scored Yes = 1 / No = 0 and drive the screen; the other four (items 7, 13, 15, 17) are conditionally displayed follow-ups that are not scored (item 7 is a numeric entry for how much weight has been lost; items 13, 15, and 17 are free text).\n3. Rating items scored 0 to 6 (items 19 to 29, 11 items): the severity-of-impact index (items 19 to 20) and the three subscale scores (items 21 to 29; 3 items each).\nRecall periods are not fixed and vary by item, spanning current, past-month, and past-3-month timeframes.\n",
    "source_url": null
   },
   {
    "title": "Why doesn't the PARDI-AR-Q give a total score?",
    "text": "The three ARFID subscales are distinct and can occur in any combination, so combining them into a single score would obscure the distinction the measure exists to draw. A child can score high on sensory-based avoidance and low on the other two, and that subscale means something different from a child with the reverse pattern. For that reason the subscales are reported and read separately rather than combined.\n",
    "source_url": null
   }
  ],
  "reportScoringGuidance": [
   {
    "documentSha256": "9dc10e566cafe883b215cefdffe65b9a6e65fb340d2568b9f49fc6b571d06359",
    "pageNumber": 2,
    "text": "The PARDI-AR-Q comprises 29 respondent-completed items (items 4–32 of the original\ninstrument) that fall into three blocks:\n1. Anthropometric items (items 4-5): height and weight (2 items). Together with age and sex,\nthese produce BMI and growth status.\n2. Diagnostic items (items 6 to 21): twelve are scored Yes = 1 / No = 0 and drive the screen, and\nthe other four (items 10, 16, 18, 20) are conditionally displayed follow-ups that are not scored —\nitem 10 is a numeric entry (how much weight has been lost), and items 16, 18, and 20 are free\ntext.\n3. Rating items scored 0 to 6 (items 22 to 32, 11 items): includes the severity-of-impact index\n(items 22 to 23, rated 0 = no difficulty, and 6 = extremely difficult) and the three subscale scores\n(items 24 to 32; 3 items each).\nRecall periods are not fixed and vary by item, spanning current, past-month, and past-3-month\ntimeframes.\nPARDI-AR-Q Scoring",
    "usage": "source_guidance_for_review_not_executable_scoring",
    "runtimeEnabled": false
   }
  ],
